r as  the main compensatory  player for decreased caloric intake  leptin stored in fat cells is our  satiety hormone  and signals to us when we feel full when  we lose weight  these fat cells shrink and leptin levels  drop meaning  our normal healthy fullness signals  become silenced  as a result we feel the need to eat more  to feel full as our body  fights to regain its preferred weight  now ghrelin  our hunger hormone is inversely related  to caloric  intake this means when we eat fewer  calories than what our body needs  ghrelin revs up and increases our  appetite  studies show that people who try to lose  weight and keep it off  end up producing more ghrelin than they  did before losing weight  then cortisol also known as the stress  hormone  is often released under severe caloric  restriction  or excessive exercise increased cortisol  can slow metabolism  and impair the ability to sustainably  lose weight  some studies have even shown that women  who consume  low calorie diets have higher cortisol  levels  and report more feelings of stress  compared to women who do not restrict  their diet  in addition when we experience an  increase in cortisol  we also experience an increase in our  insulin resistance  this would mean that cells become less  sensitive to insulin  resulting in higher blood sugar levels  which we know is a risk factor for type  2 diabetes  all of these compensatory mechanisms  become common responses to severe  weight loss caloric restriction and  yo-yo dieting  as the body fights to maintain its  natural set point weight  or weight range the most common signs  and symptoms of metabolic adaptation  include things like fatigue heartburn  digestive stress  increased hunger sleep disturbances  weight gain  anxiety loss of muscle period  irregularity  and low immunity so how can  a damaged metabolism be restored back to  normal homeostasis  after a period of restriction well two  of the most common ways to restore  metabolism  are through reverse dieting and the  all-in approach  first let's talk about reverse dieting  so reverse dieting is  exactly as it sounds and is often called  the  diet after the diet it's a way of eating  that is  usually performed after a diet or a  period of restriction  this entails progressively increasing  calories  over a certain period of time to boost  up resting metabolic rate back to its  original rate the truth is that there  isn't a lot of peer-reviewed research  on reverse dieting i mean this is a  method that is largely being used in the  fitness competition and sports nutrition  area  and success is largely anecdotal  in lieu of adequate scientific research  i spoke to two  sports dietitians to get their thoughts  chelsea cross of  mc dietetics and ben sit of evolved  sport and nutrition  now under the care of a sports dietitian  like these two  a client attempting reverse dieting  would increase their calories by about  50 to 100 calories per week they would  do this until  a caloric goal weight goal or a  perceived  set point was reached there can be a lot  of experimentation involved in this so  it is best to do this  with the guidance of a dietitian but  because the progression  is so slow and calculated there is a  lower risk of  overshooting the desired weight as with  any diet that involves adding calories  after a period of restriction  benson did explain to me that it is very  important to monitor for the signs  and symptoms of refeeding syndrome now  re-feeding syndrome occurs when somebody  with a low body weight  consuming a very low calorie diet over a  sustained  period of time dramatically increases  their calories  this can cause a potentially fatal shift  in fluid and electrolyte balance  due to a spike in insulin in response to  greater caloric intake  this is often a major concern in eating  disorder recovery  but it can also actually occur after  prolonged dieting  or restrictive eating both of these  dietitians agreed that a caloric  increase  of 50 to 100 calories per week is  generally safe to prevent refeeding  syndrome  and not likely to result in extreme fat  or weight gain  however some people can tolerate a  greater amount of calories  outside of this range while others need  to be more conservative  and this is why again it is very  important to work with a professional  on your reverse dieting journey now  let's talk about the  all-in approach now the all-in approach  is a process created by dr nicola  rinaldi  which was informed by previous treatment  methods used in eating disorder recovery  it was originally developed to restore  hormonal function quickly  in order to reverse symptoms of  hypothalamic amenorrhea  such as menstrual regularity extreme  hunger  lack of libido infertility and  temperature sensitivity  the all-in approach is now often used to  restore metabolic function  albeit faster than reverse dieting one  study looked at the resting metabolism  of women who had hypothalamic amenorrhea  and then again when they recovered from  their aha and in addition to their  hormones returning to normal  the difference in metabolism was an  impressive 300 calories a day  now you might recall my videos on  stephanie bettermore's all-in journey  stephanie had a regular cycle so she  wasn't necessarily trying to use  all in for menstrual regularity as it  was originally intended she was using  all in to help her  reset some of the side effects of her  dieting past like extreme hunger  and help her settle into her set point  weight so  how do you go all in now in dr rinaldi's  book no period now what  she suggests that recovery involves  three main pillars  which she encourages people to stick to  for a minimum of six months  to see a complete recovery these  requirements include  a following her meal plan daily which  aims for a minimum of 2500 calories a  day  without a maximum and allowing yourself  to eat to full satiety  two cutting out all high intensity  exercise  and three reducing stress and making  time to relax  it's not uncommon for people who have  come off of a period of intense  restriction  to experience what we call extreme  hunger  and therefore need a lot of calories to  recover  this is your body's way of begging not  only for enough calories to return to  baseline  but also to help replenish itself after  months  or years of under fueling it since the  body is operating at a lower metabolic  rate while consuming a sub-optimal  amount of calories  once you jump up to the 2500 calorie  minimum  it's expected that you will gain weight  and fat  this is normal in fact rinaldi suggests  that weight gain tends to bring people  into a bmi of around 22 or more which  is what she calls the fertile zone  everyone's fertile weight is different  and it may in fact be a lot higher than  the weight that you aesthetically  think that you should be the weight gain  may therefore be  just a little bit or it could be a lot  you may also gain what is often termed  overshoot weight  this is a term that rinaldi herself  really dislikes  and for good reason considering the  moral weight stigma inherently attached  to it  but essentially some people who follow  the all-in approach  find that they may initially gain extra  weight  but that as their metabolism adjusts  this weight kind of  resettles back down slightly and  plateaus  while this does not happen to everyone  and a lot of people  may simply plateau at a higher weight  when they reach their true set point  if it does occur it may be for a few  different reasons one is that the  initial weight gain may cause changes in  fluid  and electrolyte storage which can cause  water retention  and bloating two is that some people  particularly those who come from a  distorted eating history may have very  high caloric needs when they begin all  in as they not only need calories for  baseline needs  but also for repairing and refueling  damaged metabolism systems  over time once the repairing is complete  these caloric needs may drop  and regulate to just cover those  baseline needs  and three at the beginning of the all-in  journey  one's metabolism will likely be pretty  slow  which can result in some quick sometimes  significant  jumps on the scale but once metabolism  catches up and adjusts to the new  incoming caloric load it's possible that  we would see  the weight come down and stabilize like  i said rinaldi  strongly dislikes even discussing  overshoot weight  since it gives the impression that it's  only okay to gain weight because  you'll lose a lot of it once things  stabilize when in fact  gaining weight even if it's a lot of  weight and not losing a single pound  may be very necessary for maintaining  health  so what are the benefits and  disadvantages of reverse dieting versus  all in  well evidence on reverse dieting and its  alleged benefits is admittedly  lacking but experts who do use this  strategy with their clients  find that the slow stepwise method to  increasing caloric intake  allows for gradual metabolic adaptation  and reduces the risk of gaining excess  fat and weight  while preserving lean muscle mass this  is largely based on anecdotal evidence  and  not from actual research studies the  downside is that reverse dieting  like any dieting may not be appropriate  for those struggling with disordered  eating which is  actually not uncommon for people coming  from  an extreme diet or pattern of  restriction carefully counting calories  with a goal  of metabolic restoration with minimal  weight gain  can further perpetuate distorted eating  and will not help an individual  ease back into a more flexible healthy  relationship with food and their body  there is also a concern that because the  goal of reverse dieting  is to slowly bump up caloric intake  without risking weight or fat gain an  individual may never  actually ever reach their body's true  set point weight  instead they may continue to be fueling  their body at a sub-optimal level  for their unique needs but stopping  when their goal weight or desired weight  is threatened by a further caloric  increase  the other concern is that reverse  dieting does not achieve metabolic  restoration very quickly  so for those who are suffering with the  physiological health implications of  metabolic damage like hypothalamic and  manoria  infertility thyroid dysfunction or other  hormonal irregularities  they may not achieve the timely recovery  that they really truly need in contrast  the all-in approach  is arguably the most efficient way to  restore hormonal regulation  and by default of course metabolism  following a period of restriction  this will mean improvements in  everything from sleep  menstrual cycle thyroid hunger and  fullness cues etc  it's also a more intuitive approach that  can help you reach your body's natural  set point  while relearning often for the first  time in a really long time  how to listen to your body's true hunger  and fullness cues  in my professional opinion i consider  the all-in approach to be  true recovery from restriction while  reverse dieting  can offer some significant health  benefits  it may not allow for full healing to  occur  both psychologically and in some cases  physically  in other words all in is exactly what it  sounds like when it comes to  refueling your body while reverse  dieting may not be going  all the way but some of the way at a  much slower pace  it could get you all the way but it also  could not  so how do you know if you should go all  in or reverse diet to restore metabolism  well it really depends on the goals and  the health concerns of the individual  a faster reverse diet or an all-in  approach may be beneficial for somebody  having complications with their health  due to a low caloric intake in contrast  a slow meticulous reverse diet may be  beneficial for those who  want to increase their caloric intake  while minimizing fat gain  and maintaining muscle mass as best as  they can i would argue that a reverse  diet may not be suitable for anybody  with a past or present history of  disordered eating who  really should be focusing on regaining  body trust  not on another weight driven diet even  if it is one that involves  adding calories to the diet some people  may choose an  all-in approach if they are looking to  balance and restore their hormones  metabolism and their general health at a  much faster rate  while also of course working on the  relationship with food and their body  whichever one you choose again it is so  important  that you work with a registered  dietitian to ensure that this is done  safely  and properly especially if you're coming  out from a very  restrictive low-calorie diet other than  increasing caloric intake through all-in  or reverse dieting  there are a few additional ways to  attempt to improve metabolism  number one is reducing intense exercise  we know that exercise is so important  but it can become problematic when it's  excessive not supported by adequate  caloric intake  or affecting you mentally intense  exercise will not only burn the calories  that you're carefully trying to add  during metabolic restoration but it can  also increase  stress hormones like cortisol cortisol  like we talked about  high cortisol has been shown to increase  insulin and blood glucose levels  which in turn has been linked to  increased risk of obesity  and slowed metabolism so pull back on  any long or intense cardio sessions  and stick to gentle strength training  yoga stretching  and easy walks instead number two is  getting more sleep  sleep allows our body to repair itself  physically and mentally  research suggests that when sleep is  restricted cortisol levels rise which  does tend to play a role in metabolism  and weight gain over time  skimping on sleep also seems to further  reduce  leptin and increase ghrelin to hormones  that are already impacted by metabolic  damage  three is reducing stress now stress can  of course be in the form of  intense exercise or lack of sleep but it  can also take  shape from other life experiences and  factors as well  all of which can increase cortisol  levels so look for ways to reduce the  stress in your life by engaging in  relaxing activities  like meditation journaling talk therapy  yoga or other acts of self-care so what  is the bottom line on reversing  metabolic damage  the truth is if you want to improve your  metabolism and  fix metabolic damage after a period of  severe caloric restriction  there are two popular methods to do so  one is the more calculated but  arguably less effective method at  achieving full metabolic restoration  reverse dieting  while the other is more aggressive but  faster and a more thorough approach  the all-in approach gaining weight and  body fat  may be part of the process regardless of  which method you choose  and it may in fact be necessary for  overall health  whichever method you choose to reverse  metabolic adaptation  it's very important that you're working  with a registered dietitian and  if you're coming from an eating disorder  history a full  medical team and that is of course to  ensure that you're not  at risk of refeeding syndrome well folks  that is all for today's video  if you liked it be sure to give it that  thumbs up leave me a comment below if  you have any big  questions that you want me to tackle  subscribe to the channel  and i'll see you next time on abby's  kitchen bye  you
 

